#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124258 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124454 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ae7e9c8-60e9-5f5e-afbf-1de6bb01bf07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Line to a Level Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "a01560be-9023-593a-87d3-7412f3b208a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3362" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y) = 6x^2+2y^2 - 2xy^2,$ find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (3,3).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y) = k$ at the point $(x_0,y_0)$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x_0,y_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0, \\: y-y_0 \\rangle  = 0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y) = (3,3)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2x{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2x{y}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(12x-2{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+(4y-4xy)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇\\,f(3,3) & =(12(3)-2(3)^{2})i+(4(3)-4(3)(3))j \\\\  & =18\\,i-24\\,j \\\\  & =⟨18,-24⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the level curve that passes through the point $(x_0,y_0)=(3,3)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(3,3)\\cdot ⟨x-3,\\,y-3⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨18,-24⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,\\,y-3⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can leave our answer in this form. Or, if we want to leave our final answer in Cartesian form, we expand the dot product and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18(x-3)-24(y-3) & =0 \\\\ 18x-24y+18 & =0 \\\\ 3x-4y+3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/10/102f55fe0add794d3ee0f9f044b5e35c4403d4f160fe36e97d737d33ef3abb68.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124259 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124455 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "f6400ab7-3684-5f0d-b8f0-1c85b0b322ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-3363" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x^2-3xy+4y=2.$ Find a tangent vector to the curve at the point $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we set $f(x,y) = x^2-3xy+4y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=2.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(1,1) = f_y(1,1) \\ \\mathbf i - f_x(1,1) \\ \\mathbf j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-3xy+4y)\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-3xy+4y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(-3x+4)\\text{ }i-(2x-3y)\\text{ }j \\\\ t(1,1) & =(-3(1)+4)\\text{ }i-(2(1)-3(1))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =1\\,i-(-1)\\,j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $\\mathbf i +\\mathbf j,$ as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/01/0114bf524d085402bd27178d687a5faf176b7147c8e8726b84042b17823b6b0b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124260 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124456 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84245283-3cf9-5889-8c42-896a0c8af291" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points on a Curve at which the Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to a Given Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "c4ca2e8a-5a5b-54c6-983d-2f5e821084ee" :question/math-academy-id "e-3364" :question/problem "At which points on the curve $6xy+3y^2 =7+ y - 3x^2$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{j}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6xy+3{y}^{2} & =7+y-3{x}^{2} \\\\ 6xy+3{y}^{2}-y+3{x}^{2} & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y) = 6xy+3y^2 - y + 3x^2 ,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=7.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(x,y) = f_y(x,y) \\ \\mathbf i - f_x(x,y) \\ \\mathbf j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf t(x,y)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6xy+3{y}^{2}-y+3{x}^{2})\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6xy+3{y}^{2}-y+3{x}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(6x+6y-1)\\,i-(6y+6x)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $\\mathbf{j},$ we must have $\\mathbf t \\cdot \\mathbf j = 0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ ((6x+6y-1)\\,i-(6y+6x)\\,j)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ -6y-6x & =0 \\\\ y & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6xy+3{y}^{2} & =7+y-3{x}^{2} \\\\ 6x(-x)+3(-x)^{2} & =7+(-x)-3{x}^{2} \\\\ -6{x}^{2}+3{x}^{2} & =7-x-3{x}^{2} \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, if $x=7,$ we get $y=-7$ and the corresponding point is $(7,-7).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373743 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124455 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b36ad97d-7477-5e7d-8ba7-295eb688f029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373745} {:answer/id #uuid "118ee41d-f5a0-5dff-b95c-7ca94836bc57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373746} {:answer/id #uuid "19b66868-06fe-5184-957b-7342b00b0910" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i-4\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373747} {:answer/id #uuid "bd3d37cc-037a-5d75-8a4d-47c54303cb93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373748} {:answer/id #uuid "0ff73071-c5ea-582c-baaf-95b7a73e5652" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19b66868-06fe-5184-957b-7342b00b0910" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i-4\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373747} :answer-field/id #uuid "8983d5c5-c4ae-595e-a0a2-63b53b664593" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "591300c7-13a2-5dd3-a27a-1d6a5e36e2a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-130192" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{2}y={y}^{2}+3.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y & ={y}^{2}+3 \\\\ {x}^{2}y-{y}^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y-{y}^{2},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=3.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(2,1)={f}_{y}(2,1)\\,i-{f}_{x}(2,1)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y-{y}^{2})\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y-{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2y)\\,i-2xy\\,j \\\\ t(2,1) & =({2}^{2}-2(1))\\,i-2(2)(1)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $2\\,i-4\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265852 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124455 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ab7ecfb-6837-5f43-a7dc-5de26787b651" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263102} {:answer/id #uuid "23c6bb01-4817-5934-b31c-f94122a313f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263311} {:answer/id #uuid "4d97af51-08fe-5c7f-90df-c14d7845e35f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263672} {:answer/id #uuid "96b21983-40a1-5c92-8a77-84386b07a32d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264290} {:answer/id #uuid "eae56032-740f-556c-b1d0-0bf3c280086c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ab7ecfb-6837-5f43-a7dc-5de26787b651" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263102} :answer-field/id #uuid "86808a14-0099-527e-bba9-8dfc049cb102" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e97b736-408e-55e1-a895-d40d15156508" :question/math-academy-id "q-130198" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)={f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)i-{f}_{x}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)j \\\\  & =(-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)i-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)j \\\\ t(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & =(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))i-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+0\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))j \\\\  & =i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $i-j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124454 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ae7e9c8-60e9-5f5e-afbf-1de6bb01bf07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Line to a Level Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "880dbacb-028a-561a-b827-9b8d23398589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,-1⟩ \\cdot ⟨x + 1, y - 1⟩ = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122475} {:answer/id #uuid "eea120f5-6b01-590f-a495-58069c019bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨5, 3⟩ \\cdot ⟨x + 1, y - 1⟩ = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122476} {:answer/id #uuid "186d36a1-ef7f-5df9-898e-5c21ca1e411f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨5, 4⟩ \\cdot ⟨x + 1, y - 1⟩ = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122477} {:answer/id #uuid "1a23c037-a94f-5827-814e-e63f6001d36c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨ - 4,-5⟩ \\cdot ⟨x + 1, y - 1⟩ = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122478} {:answer/id #uuid "2e27fd9a-d984-5263-b543-88b22a5fa2f6" :answer/t